# Code owners for the agent pipeline.
#
# NOTE ON HOW THIS ACTUALLY WORKS — the same trap as the `agent:candidate`
# label: a CODEOWNERS entry naming a team that does not exist, or that has no
# write access to this repository, is **silently ignored**. No error, no
# warning, no review requested. So `@wafflebase/maintainers` must (1) exist and
# (2) hold at least write access here. Both were verified when this file landed;
# re-verify if reviews stop being requested.
#
# There is deliberately no repo-wide `* @owner` rule. Ownership is claimed only
# where a wrong change is dangerous rather than merely wrong, so that routine
# contributions stay reviewable by anyone with write access.

# The control plane: workflow topology, job permissions, and the trusted-code
# checkout. A mistake here fails silently and can make a pull request able to
# influence its own review.
/.github/workflows/       @wafflebase/maintainers
/.github/CODEOWNERS       @wafflebase/maintainers

# The pipeline itself, including the lens rubrics — the rubrics are prompts, so
# editing them changes what the gating reviewer looks for.
/packages/pipeline/       @wafflebase/maintainers

# The shared kernel. Consumers vendor these files byte-for-byte and verify them
# by sha256, so a change here is a change in every consumer at their next bump.
/packages/kernel/         @wafflebase/maintainers

# The adoption kit: shim templates carry security-relevant configuration (the
# concurrency key that stops a fork cancelling a legitimate review panel), so a
# template edit propagates to every repo that copies it.
/adoption/                @wafflebase/maintainers
